==Match==
===Summary===
[[File:Etrusco Unico 1990 Fifa World Cup Italy Official Match Ball.jpg|thumb|right|An example of the Adidas Etrusco Unico ball used in the match]]
The 1990 final is often cited as one of the most cynical and ugliest World Cup finals.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2016/apr/06/the-fall-how-diving-became-football-worst-crime |title=The fall: how diving became football's worst crime |date=6 April 2018 |first=Alejandro |last=Chacoff |newspaper=The Guardian |access-date=1 January 2019 |archive-date=20 October 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181020043842/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2016/apr/06/the-fall-how-diving-became-football-worst-crime |url-status=live }}</ref> It was an ill-tempered game, notable for the first two sendings off in a World Cup final. Ian Morrison wrote "the game did little for football but there was one consolation: Had the Argentines lifted the World Cup – with two wins and five goals in their seven matches – it would have been a catastrophe for the game. At least their awful approach to Italia '90 had gone unrewarded."<ref name="Jones">{{cite web |url=https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-1994-04-28-sp-51467-story.html |title=Losers Cried Foul: Bad Game in '90 Cup Final Might End Up Good for Game |last=Jones |first=Grahame L. |date=28 April 1994 |website=Los Angeles Times |access-date=29 June 2019 |archive-date=30 August 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200830154155/https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-1994-04-28-sp-51467-story.html |url-status=live }}</ref> [[Gianni Brera]] wrote in his match report for [[la Repubblica]], "Mai assistito a uno strazio paragonabile a quello di Germania-Argentina" ({{Translation|'I’ve never witnessed a more agonising match than Germany v Argentina'|literal=yes|sortable=}}).<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Brera |first=Gianni |date=10 July 1990 |title=Lo strazio in finale |url=https://quotidiano.repubblica.it/edicola/searchdetail?id=http://archivio.repubblica.extra.kataweb.it/archivio/repubblica/1990/07/10/lo-strazio-in-finale.html&hl=&query=${escapeTool.url($query)}&field=nel+testo&testata=repubblica&newspaper=REP&edition=nazionale&zona=sfoglio&ref=search |journal=La Repubblica |page= |url-access=subscription}}</ref>

The West Germany team attacked relentlessly from the beginning of the match. In the 3rd minute, [[Rudi Völler]], who had been forced off with a leg injury in the semi-final against England,<ref name="vollersinjury">{{cite news |title=England's penalty heartache |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2002/jul/15/sport.comment8 |date=5 July 1990 |first=David |last=Lacey |newspaper=The Guardian |access-date=26 December 2022}}</ref> had the first clear-cut opportunity to score from close range following a [[Free kick (association football)|free-kick]] cross by [[Andreas Brehme]], but his off-balance [[toe punt]] went off target. West Germany won another free kick in a more dangerous position two minutes later when [[Pierre Littbarski]] was fouled in the [[Penalty area|penalty arc]]. Brehme's shot hit the wall and [[Klaus Augenthaler]]'s follow-up long-range strike was saved by Argentine goalkeeper [[Sergio Goycochea]]. In the 8th and 12th minutes, Völler's header and Littbarski's [[Curl (association football)|curl shot]] went high and wide of the goal, respectively. In the 13th minute, Völler failed to receive Brehme's [[Cross (association football)#Outswinging cross|outswinging cross]], and the ball supposedly struck the arm of the Argentina defender [[Oscar Ruggeri]]. The Mexican referee [[Edgardo Codesal]] refused to award a [[Penalty kick (association football)|penalty kick]] despite [[Jürgen Klinsmann]]'s appealing for handball.<ref name="1990FIFAWCFinalMatch">{{cite AV media|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EpEPMlkpUyQ |title=West Germany 1–0 Argentina Extended Highlights 1990 FIFA World Cup|publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association|date=8 July 2020|type=YouTube video}}{{cbignore}}</ref> Five minutes later, Völler appeared to be taken down in the Argentine penalty area, but Codesal indicated to play on.<ref name="1990WCFinalMatch">{{cite AV media|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ptuW0qA2m4c&t|title=Germany 1 x 0 Argentina 1990 World Cup Final Extended Goals & Highlights HD|publisher=Rptimao TV|date=9 May 2021|type=YouTube video}}{{cbignore}}</ref> In the 23rd minute, West German captain [[Lothar Matthäus]]'s cross found Völler and his header was again wide of the target. In the 38th minute, Argentina gained a dangerous free kick when [[José Basualdo]] was fouled by [[Guido Buchwald]]. Argentina's captain [[Diego Maradona]]'s kick went up and over the wall but couldn't dip back to be on target. At half-time, the score was still level at 0–0.

The West Germans had a few chances at the start of the second half. Littbarski cut inside, [[dribbling]] past three South American defenders, but his shot from outside the box went just wide. Later, [[Thomas Berthold]] and Rudi Völler, respectively, failed to capitalize from dangerous free kicks taken by Andreas Brehme. In the 58th minute, Argentine goalkeeper Sergio Goycochea blatantly took down Klaus Augenthaler inside the penalty area, but the referee Edgardo Codesal again refused to award a penalty kick.<ref name="Germany finally awarded penatyy">{{cite book |title=The Story of the World Cup |publisher=Faber and Faber |last=Glanville |first=Brian |year=2018 |pages=326 |isbn=978-0-571-32556-6 |quote=After half-time, the game grew harsher, when Klaus Augenthaler was blantanly tripped in the box by Goycoecha, Germany had far stronger claims for a penalty than that which won the match. Sensini bought down Völler in the area Codesal gave a penalty, Argentina protested furiously, and seemed to have a pretty good case. }}</ref> [[Pedro Monzón]] had the distinction of being the first player to be sent off at a FIFA World Cup final, after being shown a straight red card for a reckless studs up challenge on Jürgen Klinsmann; FIFA had warned its officials to enforce the rules and Monzón had raised his foot during the tackle, a foul that left a {{convert|15|cm|in|0|adj=on}} gash on Klinsmann's shin.<ref>{{cite web |title=Klinsmann: the rise...and the falls |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2004/mar/07/newsstory.sport9 |publisher=Guardian News and Media |date=7 March 2004 |access-date=21 June 2014 |archive-date=12 June 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180612140755/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2004/mar/07/newsstory.sport9 |url-status=live }}</ref> In the 78th minute, after an incorrectly given [[corner kick]], West German captain Matthäus lost the ball inside his own penalty area and then appeared to trip [[Gabriel Calderón]]. Codesal once again said to play on, amid penalty shouts from the Argentinian midfielder.<ref>Brian Glanville, The story of the World Cup: The essential Guide to South Africa, 2010, pp. 325 to 327.</ref>

Six minutes from full time, Codesal incurred the wrath of the Argentinians after awarding West Germany a questionable<ref name="Germany finally awarded penatyy"/><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.infobae.com/deportes-2/mundial-2018/2018/06/07/final-italia-90-argentina-alemania-penal-codesal-sensini-brehme/ |title=Por qué el penal más polémico de los Mundiales fue un error en Italia 1990 y hoy no sería cuestionado |last=Scime |first=Miguel |date=7 June 2018 |website=Infobae |language=es |access-date=29 June 2019 |archive-date=30 August 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200830153457/https://www.infobae.com/deportes-2/mundial-2018/2018/06/07/final-italia-90-argentina-alemania-penal-codesal-sensini-brehme/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="Jones"/> penalty kick for [[Roberto Sensini]]'s [[sliding tackle]] on Völler. Regular penalty taker Matthäus had been forced to replace his [[Football boot|boots]] during the match and did not feel comfortable in the new ones,<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/matthaus-was-afraid-dropping-the-cup-514069 |title=Matthaus: I was afraid of dropping the Trophy |date=3 May 2007 |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |website=FIFA.com |access-date=29 June 2019 |quote=In the first half, I was having some problems with my boots. The sole cracked and I had to play the second half with completely new boots, which I'd never worn before. They were a completely new model. I always preferred well worn-in boots, but I didn't have a second pair with me. You don't think about things like that. Then the man from adidas came up to me and said, "This is the only pair we've got," so I said 'ok' because all I wanted to do was get back out there and play. So he gave me the boots, but I just didn't feel right in them.|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180508112322/http://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/y=2007/m=5/news=matthaus-was-afraid-dropping-the-cup-514069.html|archive-date=8 May 2018|url-status=live}}</ref> so Andreas Brehme took his place and converted the spot kick with a low, right-footed shot to the goalkeeper's right.<ref name="washingtonpost.com"/>

[[Gustavo Dezotti]], already cautioned in the first half, received a straight red card late in the match when he hauled down [[Jürgen Kohler]] with what ''The New York Times'' described as a "neck tackle right out of professional wrestling", after Kohler refused to give-up the ball in an alleged attempt to waste time. After dismissing Dezotti, Codesal was surrounded and jostled by the rest of the Argentinian team, with Maradona receiving a yellow card for dissent.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://soccernet.espn.go.com/columns/story?id=365940&root=worldcup&cc=5901 |title=World Cup 1990 – Hosts Italy |access-date=11 July 2014 |archive-date=13 October 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121013072919/http://soccernet.espn.go.com/columns/story?id=365940&root=worldcup&cc=5901 |url-status=dead }}</ref> At the final whistle, Maradona, who was man marked by [[Guido Buchwald]] for almost the entire match,<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/1990-world-cup-a-magical-night-in-rome-2402993|title=A magical night in Rome|publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |website=FIFA.com|access-date=22 May 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180518115213/https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/1990-world-cup-a-magical-night-in-rome-2402993|archive-date=18 May 2018|url-status=live}}</ref> burst into tears and blamed the referee for the loss.<ref name="washingtonpost.com">[https://web.archive.org/web/20140714153923/http://www.washingtonpost.com/sports/dcunited/argentina-germany-have-rich-world-cup-history/2014/07/11/2b3c1b6e-092f-11e4-ba5b-b9d8a4daba13_story.html The Washington Post]</ref> Argentina entered the game with four players suspended and ended it with nine men on the field, overall losing over half their squad due to injury or suspension.<ref name="glanville">{{cite book |last=Glanville |first=Brian |title=The Story of the World Cup |page=303 |publisher=Faber |year=2005 |isbn=0-571-22944-1 }}</ref><ref name="ugly">{{cite news |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1990/07/09/sports/sports-of-the-times-winning-ugly-losing-ugly-just-plain-ugly.html |title=Winning Ugly, Losing Ugly, Just Plain Ugly |work=[[The New York Times]] |date=9 July 1990 |first=George |last=Vecsey |archive-date=5 May 2019 |access-date=4 February 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190505022949/https://www.nytimes.com/1990/07/09/sports/sports-of-the-times-winning-ugly-losing-ugly-just-plain-ugly.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="class">{{Cite news |title=A poor display bare of class |newspaper=[[The Times]] |location=London |date=9 July 1990 }}</ref>

In total, West Germany had 16 scoring chances out of 23 shots. German head coach Franz Beckenbauer said "There were no doubts whatsoever who was going to win. For 90 minutes we attacked Argentina and there was no feeling of any danger that a goal would be scored against us. As I saw it, we outplayed them from beginning to end." Beckenbauer said that the penalty "was not the key to the game because in any case we would have scored, even if it had taken overtime... 1–0 by a penalty doesn't give a fair idea of this game. We could have won, 3–0. I don't remember a single chance Argentina had to score a goal."<ref name="Jones"/>

Argentina became the competition's first finalist not to score, with only one shot on goal.<ref name="washingtonpost.com"/> The South Americans failed to put together a coherent attacking strategy and lost the ball frequently. Instead, they focused on defending at all costs, knowing they would have the advantage if they managed to reach the [[Penalty shoot-out (association football)|penalty shoot-out]], as they had already advanced twice in the tournament by this means.<ref name="glanville"/><ref name="ugly"/><ref name="class"/> At the time, the 1990 final was the lowest-scoring final in the history of the competition—although this record was broken [[1994 FIFA World Cup final|four years later]], when [[Brazil national football team|Brazil]] beat [[Italy national football team|Italy]] on penalties after 120 goalless minutes.

The 1990 victory gave West Germany their third FIFA World Cup title, also making them the team to have played in the most FIFA World Cup finals at the time (three wins, three defeats), as well as avenging their defeat at the hands of Argentina in the [[1986 FIFA World Cup final|previous final]]. It also meant that Germany coach [[Franz Beckenbauer]] became the only person to have won both silver and gold medals at the World Cup as a player ([[1966 FIFA World Cup final|1966]], [[1974 FIFA World Cup final|1974]]) and as a coach (1986, 1990), and he also won a bronze medal as a player (1970). Having won on penalties against England in the semi-finals, West Germany became the first team to have won by that method en route to the title. This was repeated by Brazil (1994), [[France national football team|France]] ([[1998 FIFA World Cup knockout stage#Italy vs France|1998]]), Italy ([[2006 FIFA World Cup final|2006]]), and Argentina (twice in [[2022 FIFA World Cup knockout stage|2022]], including the [[2022 FIFA World Cup final|final]]).

